content.content:
Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
I reviewed PR #1621 (`fix/provider-honesty`) at high effort. There are 10 findings, all unverified as the high-effort recipe specifies: no verify pass ran and no tests were run. The two most likely to bite are the first two below.

- **Cost of confirming writes on ZHA and zwave-js-ui:** most User Code locks don't supervise their writes, so nearly every set or clear will now trigger a re-read of every managed slot over the radio.
- **Race in the confirmation look:** a clear can be dropped before the lock is ever read back, so an unmanaged slot keeps showing the code that was just cleared.

The deeper fix for the clear path (finding 5) is to have the clear return a result the base records, the same way the set path already does. That would remove the timestamp workaround the race depends on.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js_ui.py",
    "line": 353,
    "summary": "Unsupervised writes (most User Code locks) now return OPTIMISTIC on zwave-js-ui and ZHA. Each one starts a confirmation look whose refresh re-reads every managed slot plus the pending one, because neither provider has a cache, so every write costs a walk of the whole lock.",
    "failure_scenario": "A zwave-js-ui FLiRS lock with 20 managed slots and no Supervision support, syncing 20 slots: each set or clear goes OPTIMISTIC and record_write/record_clear start a look. async_hard_refresh_codes -> async_get_usercodes(managed | pending) sends one sendCommand per managed slot, so a bulk sync turns into hundreds of mesh round trips. The look holds the lock's turn for a whole walk, the sync's own writes queue behind it and hit LockBusy, and on a marginal link the walk times out. ZHA locks that reply without a status (zha.py:329/367) do the same over Zigbee. Before this PR both providers confirmed directly with no read."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 543,
    "summary": "The confirmation look fixes its slot scope before waiting for the lock's turn, then drops every pending clear after the read. A clear recorded while the look was waiting is discarded without ever being read back.",
    "failure_scenario": "Two unmanaged slots are released on ZHA or zwave-js-ui (base async_release_managed_slot clears each in turn). Clear 1 returns unconfirmed: record_clear starts a look task, which takes the pending set {slot1} and waits for the turn. Clear 2 gets the turn first and record_clear adds slot 2 as pending. The look then reads managed + {slot1} only. The comprehension keeps only sets, so slot 2's clear is dropped unread and no further look is scheduled. Slot 2 keeps showing the code that was just cleared (and can resurface as an unmanaged code) until the hourly drift check, which defeats _record_unconfirmed_clear's stated purpose."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js.py",
    "line": 916,
    "summary": "The new 'Available slot reads empty' branch is gated on _uc_expects_pin, but coordinator.desired_credential only consults the coordinator's first config entry. On a lock shared by two entries, slots of the second entry never count as 'expects a PIN', so the #863 stale-Available guard is skipped for them and now applies to every userCode update too.",
    "failure_scenario": "A lock is managed by entries E1 (which created the coordinator) and E2, and E2's slot 5 is active with PIN 1234. The lock sends the stale Available that #863 guards against, then a userCode update. _uc_expects_pin(5) checks only E1's config and returns False, so _handle_uc_code_update pushes empty for slot 5. E2's sync sees the slot empty, re-sets it, the next stale report empties it again, and the slot loops: exactly the sync loop the #863 guard exists to stop. Previously only the status handler had this gap; the code-update path read the value as a code."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1619,
    "summary": "When a CLEAR event is judged stale because its user index now belongs to another LCM user holding a PIN, the handler returns early. The clear is then never applied to the slot that actually lost the credential, and the _slot_by_user_index anchor fallback is skipped.",
    "failure_scenario": "Someone deletes slot A's user at the lock (out of band), and LCM then adds slot B, which the lock gives the freed index X. The dispatch for A's CLEAR now waits for the lock's turn behind B's write, so its read sees X owned by B with a PIN and the event is skipped. Nothing ever pushes empty to slot A, which keeps showing an occupied credential (and can stay 'in sync' for an inactive slot or block reallocation) until a drift refresh."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1582,
    "summary": "Root-cause altitude: an unconfirmed clear is signalled by a side effect inside the provider (_record_unconfirmed_clear). The base then has to stamp the turn-acquire time through a pre_execute lambda into a one-element list so that drop_superseded_by_clear doesn't drop the record the provider just made. The set path instead returns a WriteResult and the base records it.",
    "failure_scenario": "Have async_delete_credential and async_clear_usercode return a result (CONFIRMED/OPTIMISTIC/NO_CHANGE) and let async_internal_clear_usercode call record_clear itself, mirroring async_internal_set_usercode. That removes the started[] timestamp, the written_at comparison in drop_superseded_by_clear, and the turn-ordering subtlety, which is also where finding 2's race lives. As written, every future provider must remember to call the hook from inside its write, and any clear path that bypasses async_internal_clear_usercode skips the supersede logic entirely."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1613,
    "summary": "On a polled (api-only) zwave-js-ui lock, an unconfirmed clear is read back twice: record_clear starts a confirmation look, and async_internal_clear_usercode still calls coordinator.async_request_refresh() because the clear reported changed=True.",
    "failure_scenario": "A zwave-js-ui gateway with a custom topic (supports_push False) receives an unsupervised clear. The look reads managed + pending slots, and the debounced refresh reads every managed slot again. That doubles per-slot sendCommand traffic per clear for no added information, since the set path already skips the refresh when the write is pending."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1589,
    "summary": "Every Matter LockUserChange event now takes a full rate-limited turn to run get_lock_users, which serializes it behind (and ahead of) sync writes. That includes the events Lock Code Manager's own writes generate, whose outcome it has already pushed.",
    "failure_scenario": "During a bulk Matter sync, each credential add emits a PIN ADD event. Each event's dispatch queues for the turn, applies _min_operation_delay, and reads every user, so roughly one extra full user read per write is inserted between the sync's writes. That slows the sync and raises the chance that writes hit LockBusy, while the read only confirms a value LCM already pushed."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/sync.py",
    "line": 926,
    "summary": "The suspension reason is picked from the current active_state, but the slot breaker isn't reset when the target flips while OUT_OF_SYNC or PENDING_CONFIRMATION. Mixed set and clear failures can therefore be reported as 'N attempts to clear it'.",
    "failure_scenario": "An active slot fails two sets (two charges). The user turns it off: the pending set is dropped as stale with no breaker reset. One clear is then charged and the breaker trips at 3, so the repair says the lock 'still held its code after 3 attempts to clear it' when only one clear was ever attempted."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/strings.json",
    "line": 422,
    "summary": "The 'sync will resume…' guidance was moved out of the translatable description into hard-coded English f-strings repeated at four _suspend_slot call sites. Persistent slot_suspended issues created before the upgrade keep their old reason placeholder, so they now show no resume guidance at all. The clear wording also omits that editing the PIN resumes sync.",
    "failure_scenario": "A slot suspended under the previous release persists across the upgrade (is_persistent=True). Its stored reason lacks the resume sentence, and the new description template no longer has it, so the user sees no way out. Separately, any future translation can't localize the resume text, and four copies of nearly the same sentence must be kept in step. The clear-path text says sync resumes once the slot is 'active again or the code is removed at the lock', but _code_suspend_target also resumes on a PIN change."
  },
  {
    "file": "AGENTS.md",
    "line": 329,
    "summary": "The PR adds tests/providers/zha/test_e2e.py, but AGENTS.md still says each provider package has a test_e2e.py 'except zha'. That is now stale, contrary to the repo CLAUDE.md instruction to keep AGENTS.md up to date.",
    "failure_scenario": "/Users/raman/projects/lock_code_manager/CLAUDE.md: \"Claude Code users should also reference `AGENTS.md` for complete guidance and keep it up to date as instructed.\" The diff adds tests/providers/zha/test_e2e.py, while AGENTS.md:329 reads \"Each provider package has a `conftest.py`, a `test_provider.py`, and (except `zha`) a `test_e2e.py`\". Agents relying on it will assume ZHA has no e2e suite."
  }
]
```

