    "line": 1635,
    "summary": "When the users read fails (including the new LockBusy from waiting for the lock's turn), a CLEAR event still falls back to the slot anchored to the user index and pushes it empty. That skips the new check for a stale clear whose index now belongs to someone else, even though the write that held the turn may have just re-pointed the anchor to the index's new owner.",
    "failure_scenario": "A keypad deletion frees user index I (slot X) and sends LockUserChange CLEAR. LCM's write for slot Y holds the turn, is given index I, and line 694 sets _slot_by_user_index[I]=Y. The event read then waits behind that write or a walk longer than wait_budget, so LockBusy is raised. The fallback pops anchor I, gets Y, and pushes Y empty: a false clear on a slot that holds a PIN, plus Y's anchor is lost. The stale-clear guard at 1621 only runs when the read succeeds."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 319,
    "summary": "set and clear treat any reply that has a `status` field as this command's answer. The get path now checks that the reply is a get_pin_code_response for the right slot, but set and clear never check the reply's schema or command_id.",
    "failure_scenario": "The branch's own premise is that zigpy pairs replies by transaction sequence number. A status-bearing reply that belongs to something else (for example a Default_Response for another command_id) can fill the set_pin_code request. With status 0 the write is CONFIRMED and pushed known(pin) though the lock never confirmed it. With a non-zero status, CodeRejectedError is raised and sync disables the slot."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/sync.py",
    "line": 937,
    "summary": "The suspension reason for an inactive slot says the lock 'still held its code' and that the code 'may still open the door'. But the strikes on an inactive slot also come from LockOperationFailed raised by the clear itself, when no read ever showed a code.",
    "failure_scenario": "On an inactive slot, three clears raise LockOperationFailed (for example a Matter UnknownError or another transport-level failure classed as an operation failure). Each charges the slot breaker at the LockOperationFailed handler. The repair then tells the user the lock still held its code after 3 attempts, which no read established. This is a clear-only path, separate from the mixed set/clear wording deferred to PR C."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 188,
    "summary": "desired_credential (and is_slot_managed, which sets `chargeable`) now goes through find_entry_for_lock_slot. That uses async_entries(DOMAIN), which includes disabled and not-loaded entries, so a slot claimed only by a disabled entry counts as wanted or managed.",
    "failure_scenario": "A disabled entry still lists lock L, slot 4, enabled with a PIN, and a loaded entry shares L. Z-Wave JS then ignores real Available reports for slot 4 (_uc_expects_pin is True). A clear of slot 4 on ZHA or zwave-js-ui is recorded chargeable=True, and its failure goes into _failed_writes with no sync manager to take it. When the entry is re-enabled, the first tick is charged a strike for a clear it never made."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 547,
    "summary": "The new filter says a pending clear the look 'was not asked about waits for the next look'. But _apply_read has already settled, and possibly charged, every pending clear the read names, including clears recorded while the look waited that are outside `asked`.",
    "failure_scenario": "A provider whose scoped refresh answers some slots from a cache instead of the device returns the old cached code for a clear recorded during the look's wait. _apply_read then charges a clear the read never asked the lock about. This is latent today, since ZHA re-reads unverified managed slots and zwave-js-ui walks them, but the invariant lives only in a comment. _apply_read should skip clears whose user_ref is not in `asked`."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1600,
    "summary": "The new early return for a failed read on a non-empty event is dead logic: with code_slot None and resolved non-empty, control already reaches the 'did not resolve' return below. The success path also scans raw_users twice, once in next() for `owner` and again in _lcm_slot_from_raw_users_by_user_index.",
    "failure_scenario": "It adds a branch and a comment that change only which debug line is logged, and duplicates a list scan. Deriving code_slot from owner['user_name'] via parse_tag removes the second pass."
  },
  {
    "file": "AGENTS.md",
    "line": 462,
    "summary": "CLAUDE.md says AGENTS.md must be kept up to date ('reference AGENTS.md for complete guidance and keep it up to date as instructed'). The 'Writes, and how they are confirmed' section still says 'A provider never tracks pending writes itself' and documents only set outcomes.",
    "failure_scenario": "Providers now record unconfirmed clears themselves via _record_unconfirmed_clear (ZHA, zwave-js-ui), and async_delete_credential has a new unconfirmed-clear contract. A provider author following AGENTS.md would push empty after an unconfirmed clear and skip the read-back and breaker charge the branch relies on."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 188,
    "summary": "desired_credential used to be an O(1) lookup in the coordinator's own entry. It now walks every Lock Code Manager config entry, building EntryConfig.from_entry for any that are not loaded, on every call.",
    "failure_scenario": "It runs on every code sensor native_value for an unreadable slot (every Matter slot, on every state write) and on every Z-Wave userCode value update, where _uc_expects_pin is now called unconditionally. That is repeated entry scans and config construction on hot paths, where a lock and slot to entry index, or reusing is_slot_managed's lookup, would do."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 242,
    "summary": "A pending clear is settled, uncharged, by any unreadable read. On ZHA a per-slot mesh timeout is reported as unreadable, so an unconfirmed clear followed by a timed-out read-back ends with last_write_was_clear reporting the slot in sync.",
    "failure_scenario": "A ZHA lock drops a clear (reply without status), and the one-slot confirmation read times out on a lossy mesh, giving unreadable(). The clear is settled, nothing is charged, the inactive slot reads as in sync, and the revoked code stays on the door with no further retries. This is the clear-side counterpart of the PR E (set) deferral; skip it if PR E covers clears too."
  }
]
