toolUseResult.result:
I found 9 issues. The most important one answers your pre-existing question: the branch does not change it, and its new Working rationale only holds when zwave-js-ui receives push updates (push mode).

**The pre-existing question.** On an api-only zwave-js-ui lock (`supports_push` False), a Success or Working write returns `CONFIRMED`. The base set path then calls `record_write(believed=False)`, and the confirmation look reads the slot back straight away through `async_get_usercodes`, which asks the device. Main did exactly this for every api-only set, so this is not a regression.

The new `_supervised_write_result` docstring says Working is believed because "a read made while the lock works still finds the old code". On api-only that early read still happens. When the slot held a different readable code, it counts the write as failed and the slot gets a strike. Push mode is fine: there `CONFIRMED` takes the `drop_pending` path and nothing is read back.

The out-of-scope items you listed are excluded.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1542,
    "summary": "Pre-existing and not changed by this branch: on an api-only (polled) zwave-js-ui lock, a Supervision Working set returns CONFIRMED and is still recorded pending (believed=False) and read back immediately, which contradicts the new _supervised_write_result rationale.",
    "failure_scenario": "Api-only zwave-js-ui lock. The user changes slot 3 from PIN A to PIN B. The lock answers Supervision Working. The base calls record_write(B, believed=False), the immediate look reads the device while it is still working and gets readable A. _apply_read sees a readable code that is not the written one, adds the slot to _failed_writes, and the next tick charges a strike and re-sends B. This is the outcome the docstring says Working avoids; it only does so in push mode, where CONFIRMED takes the drop_pending path."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1385,
    "summary": "MatterLock.async_unload cancels the dispatches that exist when it starts, then awaits (the gather, then super's reconnect-task await) before super unsubscribes push, so LockUserChange events arriving in that window create new dispatch tasks that are never cancelled.",
    "failure_scenario": "The lock is unloaded while it is emitting events (for example right after a write). An event lands during `await asyncio.gather(...)` or the reconnect-task await. `_on_node_event` creates a new dispatch after the cancel loop. That dispatch then runs `_execute_rate_limited`/`_raw_lock_users` against a provider being torn down and calls `_confirm_slot` on a coordinator that has shut down, which the commit set out to prevent. Unsubscribing push first, or cancelling after super().async_unload, would close the window."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1625,
    "summary": "Every LockUserChange dispatch now does a full get_lock_users read inside the lock's turn and pays the minimum delay between operations, including the events Lock Code Manager's own writes generate, so a sync of N slots becomes N writes interleaved with about N to 2N full user-list reads, one at a time.",
    "failure_scenario": "Startup sync of 20 Matter slots. Each set emits LockUserChange (user and PIN), and each dispatch queues a `get_lock_users` holder behind the writes and the confirmation look, adding at least MIN_OPERATION_DELAY each. Sync takes far longer, and a sync tick or look queued behind several dispatch reads can exceed the one-holder wait budget and get LockBusy. The read is wasted for Lock Code Manager's own writes, which already pushed their outcome."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js.py",
    "line": 915,
    "summary": "_handle_uc_code_update now reports a slot empty whenever the cached userIdStatus is Available and no PIN is wanted, ignoring the code value. The comment itself says the driver caches a stale Available as readily as a true one, and the later Enabled status update is ignored ('Occupied statuses carry no code').",
    "failure_scenario": "Slot 4 is inactive (no PIN wanted). Its cached status is a stale Available, and a userCode value update arrives carrying a real code that was not preceded by a status update (an optimistic setValue from another controller, or the lock re-reporting a code a clear did not remove). The provider confirms the slot empty, which also settles a pending clear uncharged, so Lock Code Manager believes the revoked code is gone while it still opens the door, until the next drift check."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/queries.py",
    "line": 283,
    "summary": "find_managing_entry treats entries in UNLOAD_IN_PROGRESS or NOT_LOADED as not managing, so desired_credential returns empty for a slot whose entry is mid-reload. During that window the zwave_js and zwave_js_ui stale-Available guards (#863) stop applying to that entry's slots.",
    "failure_scenario": "An options change reloads entry B, which shares the lock with entry A's coordinator. While B is unloading or not loaded, the lock sends a stale userIdStatus=AVAILABLE for B's slot 7. `_uc_expects_pin(7)` is now False, so the provider pushes empty and data shows slot 7 empty. When B finishes loading its sync sees an empty active slot and re-writes the PIN: the loop #863 guarded against, reopened for each reload."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1404,
    "summary": "async_internal_read_back sizes its time budget from scope() before it has the lock's turn, but the read runs on scope() evaluated in the turn. A pending write to an unmanaged slot recorded while it waits adds an exchange the budget does not cover.",
    "failure_scenario": "A provider with per_exchange_budget set and a walk sized to managed slots exactly. While the look waits, a direct clear or set on an unmanaged slot records a pending write, so the walk becomes N+1 exchanges under an N-exchange budget. A slow but healthy lock times out into LockDisconnected, and `_give_up_overdue` then gives up writes past their deadline that the read would have confirmed."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 2262,
    "summary": "No production caller passes `slots` to async_internal_get_usercodes or async_internal_hard_refresh_codes any more, since the look now goes through async_internal_read_back. The parameter is dead outside tests, and the docstring ('The coordinator's confirmation read names the pending addresses too') and the widening comment in async_internal_hard_refresh_codes are stale.",
    "failure_scenario": "Maintenance cost: two code paths and their test_base cases keep a scoped-read contract nothing uses, and a reader following the docstring will look for the confirmation read in the wrong method. Drop the `slots` parameter from both wrappers, or make the look the only scoped entry point."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1406,
    "summary": "_async_refresh_from_coordinator rebuilds a full snapshot by projecting the coordinator's own data back as a read, only so the look can replace data wholesale. Merging a scoped read into data for push providers would do the same job without the projection or the 'managed slot the coordinator holds nothing for' special case.",
    "failure_scenario": "The coordinator feeds its own state through the provider and back through `_apply_read`. This adds a base helper, a special case for slots with no data, and an ordering argument ('taken after the read, so a push that landed while it walked is kept'), all to undo the replace step. Treating a push provider's look as a partial read merged over `self.data` would remove the helper and that ordering argument."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 550,
    "summary": "After _apply_read the look throws away every remaining pending clear, assuming any clear the read did not name is a slot the lock does not hold. That holds only because every provider's scoped read currently names every slot it is asked about, and nothing enforces it.",
    "failure_scenario": "A provider whose scoped read can leave out a requested slot instead of marking it unreadable (a future provider, or a change to `_async_read_slots` that skips silent slots). Its unconfirmed clear is settled uncharged without being read, and data drops the slot. A lock that ignores clears is then never charged, and the slot is never suspended, while the revoked code may still work."
  }
]
