Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
I found 8 issues on `fix/provider-honesty`; none is a crash or data-loss bug. The most important is a stale Matter "add" event that can mark a just-cleared slot as occupied. All 8 are unverified candidates: this review skipped the verify pass. The topics you listed as out of scope are excluded.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1707,
    "summary": "A LockUserChange add/modify event now pushes 'occupied' even when the read shows its tagged owner no longer holds a PIN. The new stale-event guard only covers clears.",
    "failure_scenario": "The dispatch now waits for the lock's turn before it reads users. Sequence: Lock Code Manager sets a PIN on slot S, and the PIN-add event is queued. A clear of S, queued before that event's read starts, takes the turn and pushes S empty. The user persists without a PIN under the persistent-user lifecycle. The dispatch then reads users and finds the owner tagged S with no PIN credential. It still calls _confirm_slot(S, unreadable), so coordinator data shows S occupied while the lock is empty. The inactive slot goes out of sync, the re-clear finds no owner and returns False, and the slot stays out of sync until a drift refresh. A guard like the clear one (an add/modify whose owner holds no PIN is history) is missing."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1453,
    "summary": "_async_refresh_from_coordinator replays coordinator data as if it were a read from the lock. That data can still hold a believed optimistic PIN whose write was given up, so ZHA confirmation looks now keep an unverified value instead of re-reading it.",
    "failure_scenario": "On ZHA, a set answered by a SUCCESS Default Response is recorded with its PIN believed, and data shows known(pin). The read-backs raise LockDisconnected until the deadline. _give_up_overdue then pops the pending write but leaves known(pin) in data. The tick charges one strike, then treats the slot as in sync because data matches. On main, the next confirmation look for any other slot re-read every managed slot and corrected this. Now later looks read only pending slots and project known(pin) back through _apply_read as the lock's own answer. On locks with programming events (no hourly drift refresh), a code that never landed stays reported as present indefinitely."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js_ui.py",
    "line": 362,
    "summary": "A User Code set or clear that comes back with no Supervision result is now unconfirmed. On the MQTT provider every such write triggers a confirmation look that walks every managed slot plus the written one.",
    "failure_scenario": "Older Z-Wave locks without Supervision support return no result for every write, and each was CONFIRMED on main. Now each set is recorded optimistic and each clear pending. The look's scoped refresh goes through BaseMqttLock.async_hard_refresh_codes, which deliberately reads managed_slots | slots. Editing one PIN on a battery-powered (wake-on-demand) lock with 20 managed slots now costs one write plus 20 sequential User Code Gets on the mesh, where main sent one write. node-zwave-js already verifies unsupervised writes itself."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js_ui.py",
    "line": 355,
    "summary": "On a lock reached only through the API (no push), a Supervision Working write is treated as confirmed, so the slot is read back about 2 seconds later. A PIN change still pending on the lock reads the old code and is failed and charged at once.",
    "failure_scenario": "The slot holds 1111 and the user changes it to 2222. The lock answers Working with a remaining duration of a few seconds. Because the lock does not push, the base records the write pending with believed=False. The immediate look reads 1111 before the lock applies the change. _apply_read takes a readable different code as an immediate failure, without waiting for the deadline, so the slot breaker is charged for a write the lock promised to apply. Three such edits within five minutes suspend the slot. The docstring acknowledges this case, but nothing defers the judgment."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1416,
    "summary": "async_internal_read_back computes scope() twice per look (once for the exchanges budget, once in pre_execute) and calls managed_slots up to three times. Each call scans every config entry.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every confirmation look on a polled lock runs _look_scope twice, and each run calls get_managed_slots over all Lock Code Manager entries, plus a third managed_slots call in the exchanges lambda. Nothing can change scope between those two synchronous points, so computing it once and reusing it for the budget would do the same job without the repeated scans."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 621,
    "summary": "When a failed read gives up an optimistic write past its deadline, the believed value is left in data as though the lock had reported it. No later path re-reads that slot.",
    "failure_scenario": "An optimistic (believed) set is followed by read-backs that raise until the deadline. _fail_overdue pops the pending write and adds a failed write, but data keeps SlotCredential.known(pin). The next tick charges one strike, then calculate_in_sync sees a verified known(pin) that matches and reports the slot in sync, so no re-sync happens. The underlying fix is in the mechanism: revert or mark the believed value as unconfirmed when a believed write is given up. This also removes the hazard in the projection finding above."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 143,
    "summary": "A new docstring uses the abbreviation 'LCM', which breaks the project rule to spell out acronyms in code comments.",
    "failure_scenario": "/Users/raman/projects/lock_code_manager/CLAUDE.md names AGENTS.md as the master guidance file, and AGENTS.md (Code Style) says: \"**Spell out acronyms** in code comments instead of using abbreviations.\" The added line is `\"\"\"Return the LCM slot a raw lock user's tag names, or ``None`` if untagged.\"\"\"`."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/sync.py",
    "line": 865,
    "summary": "A new comment uses the abbreviation 'PIN' where the project rule asks for acronyms to be spelled out. The adjacent new code in the diff uses 'Personal Identification Number'.",
    "failure_scenario": "Same AGENTS.md rule, referenced from /Users/raman/projects/lock_code_manager/CLAUDE.md: \"**Spell out acronyms** in code comments instead of using abbreviations.\" The added comment reads `# the desired state: the PIN for an active slot, a clear (no PIN)`. The new zwave_js.py docstring `\"\"\"Return whether Lock Code Manager wants a PIN on a User Code slot.\"\"\"` has the same issue."
  }
]
