    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1416,
    "summary": "async_internal_read_back computes scope() twice per look (once for the exchanges budget, once in pre_execute) and calls managed_slots up to three times. Each call scans every config entry.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every confirmation look on a polled lock runs _look_scope twice, and each run calls get_managed_slots over all Lock Code Manager entries, plus a third managed_slots call in the exchanges lambda. Nothing can change scope between those two synchronous points, so computing it once and reusing it for the budget would do the same job without the repeated scans."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 621,
    "summary": "When a failed read gives up an optimistic write past its deadline, the believed value is left in data as though the lock had reported it. No later path re-reads that slot.",
    "failure_scenario": "An optimistic (believed) set is followed by read-backs that raise until the deadline. _fail_overdue pops the pending write and adds a failed write, but data keeps SlotCredential.known(pin). The next tick charges one strike, then calculate_in_sync sees a verified known(pin) that matches and reports the slot in sync, so no re-sync happens. The underlying fix is in the mechanism: revert or mark the believed value as unconfirmed when a believed write is given up. This also removes the hazard in the projection finding above."
