```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 657,
    "summary": "When a believed write is given up, `_give_up_overdue` puts back `PendingWrite.observed`, but that value is captured once in `record_write` (line 325) and never updated afterwards, so it can be older than the last read or can be a value nobody ever saw on the lock.",
    "failure_scenario": "Two ways in. (1) The slot holds 1111 and an optimistic set of 2222 is recorded with observed=1111. A look reads the slot as empty before the deadline: `_apply_read` line 270 keeps showing 2222 and leaves observed alone. The next look fails past the deadline, and `_give_up_overdue` puts back 1111, which the lock was last seen not holding. (2) A believed 2222 is dropped as stale by the sync tick (`drop_pending`), which leaves 2222 in data. The next believed write captures observed=data.get(), i.e. 2222. Give that one up and 2222 comes back, though it was never confirmed. Either way the coordinator now reports a code the lock does not hold. `_check_duplicate_code` then raises DuplicateCodeError when another slot is set to that code. On ZHA with programming events (no drift check), the push look's `_async_refresh_from_coordinator` keeps projecting the wrong value forward indefinitely."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/sync.py",
    "line": 878,
    "summary": "The restore added in 2efa1969 only runs when a believed write is given up. When a believed write is dropped as stale (`drop_pending` here, or `drop_superseded_by_clear`), its unconfirmed value stays in coordinator data as if the lock had said it.",
    "failure_scenario": "On ZHA or zwave-js-ui, an optimistic set of 2222 pushes 2222 into data. The user changes the PIN to 3333 before any read, so the tick drops the 2222 write as stale. The 3333 write then fails or is rejected. Data still shows 2222, a value no read or push ever confirmed. On a push lock with no drift check nothing re-reads that slot, so 2222 remains a phantom code: setting 2222 on another slot fails with DuplicateCodeError, and the slot's code sensor shows a code that is not on the lock."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 600,
    "summary": "`_parse_pin_response` and `_parse_write_status` (line 575) only accept a reply that is an instance of the stock `DoorLock.ClientCommandDefs.*_response.schema` class, but zigpy decodes a reply with the schema of the device's own cluster class.",
    "failure_scenario": "A quirk that redefines `client_commands` for its DoorLock cluster gets new schema classes built by `with_compiled_schema()` (checked: a plain subclass shares the stock classes, a redefined command does not). For such a lock, every get_pin_code reply fails the isinstance test and every slot reads as unreadable. Every set_pin_code or clear_pin_code reply is also rejected, so every set is OPTIMISTIC and every clear is recorded unconfirmed, including outright rejections that should raise CodeRejectedError. Comparing `result.command.id` (or the command name) instead of the class identity avoids this."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1724,
    "summary": "The new stale add/modify guard drops any PIN add or modify event whose owner, in the later user read, has no `credentials` entry with type 'pin'. That assumes `get_lock_users` always fills in credentials, and it also drops genuine add events that a later, unrelated clear has overtaken.",
    "failure_scenario": "If `get_lock_users` returns a tagged user with `credentials: None` or an empty list (for example a read that omits credential details, or a credential read that failed inside the SDK helper), every LockUserChange ADD or MODIFY for that user is logged as stale and ignored. Before this branch it was pushed as unreadable. An out-of-band PIN add on a Lock Code Manager user then stays invisible until the next drift read."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 254,
    "summary": "Altitude: how a clear is judged is now restated as `pending.pin is None` branches in `_apply_read`, `observe_push`, `_fail_overdue`, the look's pending filter, `drop_superseded_by_clear` and the sync tick's `desired_pin`. That is six places that each spell out the same rule.",
    "failure_scenario": "The read-side and push-side twins already have to be kept in step by hand (the docstrings cross-reference each other). Several fixes on this branch were exactly one site disagreeing with another. A `PendingWrite` holding the desired `SlotCredential` (empty for a clear) plus a single `judge(observed)` method would remove the copied conditions, and the next state added would not need six edits."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/queries.py",
    "line": 117,
    "summary": "`find_managing_entry` re-implements `find_entry_for_lock_slot` (line 80) with a `_keeps_its_slots` filter added, and the old function is still used by `async_fire_code_slot_event` (`_base.py` ~2432). The two lookups now disagree about disabled and deleted entries.",
    "failure_scenario": "A slot owned by a disabled entry counts as unmanaged for `desired_credential`, `is_slot_managed` and clear charging. Yet `async_fire_code_slot_event` still attributes a keypad use on that slot to the disabled entry. Two near-identical lookups that differ only in this filter will keep drifting apart. The old one should call the new one, or be removed. Also, when `ask_first` does not match, `find_managing_entry` checks it a second time through `async_entries`."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js.py",
    "line": 955,
    "summary": "Altitude: the new Available guard `_uc_expects_pin` uses `desired_credential`, which looks only at configured enabled and PIN. The sync tick's new `desired_pin` uses the active state. So the two disagree for a slot that is enabled but inactive because of its condition.",
    "failure_scenario": "Take an enabled slot with a PIN whose condition is off. Sync clears it, since the active state is OFF. The lock reports Available, possibly with a stale code field (#819). `_uc_expects_pin` is True from config, so the new 'Available is empty' rule is skipped and the status handler ignores Available. The cleared slot keeps reading as holding a code, and the inactive slot is cleared again on every tick. One shared notion of what the slot should hold would remove the disagreement."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1383,
    "summary": "`async_internal_read_back` notices an empty scope only after `_execute_rate_limited` has run both availability probes and waited for the turn, even though the coordinator could check `_look_scope()` for emptiness first.",
    "failure_scenario": "On Schlage the probe is a cloud round trip. A look whose pending writes were all settled by pushes while it queued still pays for the probes (and a probe timeout ends as LockDisconnected, which routes into `_give_up_overdue`) before finding out there is nothing to read. Rechecking `self._pending` before the call, and keeping the in-turn check for the race, avoids the wasted I/O."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js_ui.py",
    "line": 349,
    "summary": "A docstring added by this branch uses the abbreviation 'JSON', which breaks the AGENTS.md rule (referenced from the repo-root CLAUDE.md): \"**Spell out acronyms** in code comments instead of using abbreviations.\"",
    "failure_scenario": "Convention violation in new text: \"A boolean status is no status: ``True == 1`` in Python, and a JSON ``true`` must not pass for Working.\" Commit 9905186e was meant to spell out the acronyms this branch added, and this one was missed."
  }
]
```

