```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 586,
    "summary": "(a) Introduced in 53db7114. A believed set's `observed` value is only updated when a read names its address. When a read replaces `data` without naming the address, the belief is dropped from `data` but `observed` keeps its pre-write value, so a later `_unbelieve` brings back a code the newest read showed was gone.",
    "failure_scenario": "zwave_js (push): a direct service call sets 2222 on unmanaged slot 12, which held 1111. The set returns OPTIMISTIC, so it is recorded as pending(2222, believed, observed=known(1111)). Someone deletes 1111 at the keypad and the 2222 write never lands. The hard refresh in the look is scoped to slot 12, but its projection is unscoped, so it names managed slots plus occupied unmanaged ones and slot 12 does not appear. `async_set_updated_data(new_data)` therefore drops slot 12 from `data`, while the pending write stays believed with observed=known(1111). The next look fails past the deadline and `_give_up_overdue` calls `_unbelieve`, which sets data[12]=known(1111). That code now shows as held, and `_check_duplicate_code` refuses 1111 on any other slot as a duplicate. The polled path at line 898 has the same gap for unmanaged slots, and the belief flickers out of `data` on every poll. Fix: after `_apply_read`, set `observed=None` on every believed pending set the read did not name, in both the look and the poll/drift paths."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 719,
    "summary": "(b/c) Design depth. Storing believed values in `data` (the lock's word) forces several special cases: `observed` tracking in four places, `_unbelieve` on three exit paths, and `push_update` setting believed=False on a pending write it does not otherwise touch. A belief overlay kept apart from `data` would remove all of them.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every new path that ends or replaces a believed write has to remember both to update `observed` and to call `_unbelieve`. The first finding is one such path that was missed. The flag clearing in `push_update` only exists because providers' confirmed-write pushes call `push_update` rather than `observe_push`/`drop_pending`. With `data` holding only reads and pushes, plus a `{address: pin}` overlay applied in `credential()`/`credentials_by_slot()` while a write is pending, abandoning a write is just popping the overlay. `observed`, `_unbelieve`, the push flag clearing, and the not-named gap would all disappear."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 661,
    "summary": "(a) Introduced in 53db7114. `_give_up_overdue` calls `_unbelieve(overdue)`, which already calls `async_update_listeners()` when it changes `data`, and then calls `async_update_listeners()` again unconditionally, so every slot sync manager and entity is notified twice.",
    "failure_scenario": "A believed write is given up after a read fails past its deadline. `_unbelieve` puts the observed value back and notifies all listeners. `_give_up_overdue` then notifies them all again: every SlotSyncManager runs `request_sync_check` twice and every coordinator entity writes its state twice. Fix: have `_unbelieve` return whether it notified, and notify in `_give_up_overdue` only when it did not."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 329,
    "summary": "(a) Introduced in 53db7114. When `record_write(believed=False)` replaces a believed pending write, the old believed PIN stays in `data`. The new record carries the old `observed` value but has believed=False, so `_unbelieve` skips it and nothing can take the old belief back.",
    "failure_scenario": "A polled provider returns OPTIMISTIC for PIN A (A is pushed into `data` as believed), then returns CONFIRMED for PIN B without pushing. The new record is pending(B, believed=False, observed=X). If the look's read then fails past the deadline, `_give_up_overdue` → `_unbelieve` skips the non-believed record, and `data` keeps showing A, a value the lock never confirmed. Duplicate checks and the code sensor keep A. No shipped provider does exactly this today: zwave_js_ui pushes on CONFIRMED. The invariant is still unenforced. Fix: call `_unbelieve(previous)` in `record_write` whenever the replaced record was believed and the new one is not."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1707,
    "summary": "(a) `holds_pin` counts any credential with type 'pin', including one whose `index` is None. `async_get_users` (line 540) and the anchor helpers (line 997) ignore such credentials. The new stale-event checks therefore use a different definition of 'holds a PIN' than every other Matter read.",
    "failure_scenario": "matter-server returns a user tagged `lcm:4:` with `credentials: [{\"type\": \"pin\", \"index\": null}]`. A LockUserChange CLEAR arrives for that user index. `holds_pin` is True, so the clear is dropped as stale. `async_get_users` projects the same user as holding no PIN, so slot 4 keeps whatever the coordinator last had and is never pushed empty by this event."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 620,
    "summary": "(b) Fallout of 74cf2460/7aeab776. `is_slot_managed` now uses `find_managing_entry`, which excludes disabled and deleted entries. A duplicate in a slot owned by a disabled Lock Code Manager entry is now reported as an 'unmanaged slot'.",
    "failure_scenario": "Entry A (disabled) still has PIN 1234 in slot 3 on the lock. Entry B tries to set 1234 on slot 5. `_check_duplicate_code` raises DuplicateCodeError with conflicting_slot_managed=False, so the repair reads 'PIN duplicates unmanaged slot 3'. The user is told the code is foreign when it belongs to their own disabled entry."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/queries.py",
    "line": 133,
    "summary": "(a) Simplification. The `ask_first` fast path in `_find_entry` (itertools.chain plus an identity filter, threaded through `find_managing_entry`, `desired_credential` and `is_slot_managed`) cannot change the result: the uniqueness constraint allows at most one entry per lock+slot. It saves only a scan over a handful of entries.",
    "failure_scenario": "The cost is maintenance. Three call sites have to compute and pass `ask_first` (including the `None if self.coordinator is None else self.coordinator.config_entry` ternary in `is_slot_managed`), and two public wrappers exist over one private function. Dropping `ask_first` and merging `find_entry_for_lock_slot`/`find_managing_entry` into one function with a `keeping_slots` keyword gives identical results with less code."
  }
]
```

