content.content:
Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
I found 7 findings: 3 correctness issues ranked first, then 4 cleanups. I didn't run any tests, and the worktree is unchanged. Each finding gives its class: (a) introduced by this branch, (b) fallout of an earlier fix on this branch, or (c) predates the branch.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js.py",
    "line": 927,
    "summary": "(c), but the branch rewrote this function's comment around it: when a code is expected and a stale Available report arrives with an empty or all-zeros code, the handler still resolves the slot as empty, because `_uc_slot_in_use` is False for an Available status, so the #863 guard in the status handler is bypassed.",
    "failure_scenario": "A slot is active with PIN 1234 and the lock sends the stale Available report #863 describes (userIdStatus=Available, userCode ''). The status handler correctly ignores it. The driver then writes userCode '' and `_handle_uc_code_update` runs. The new Available branch is skipped because a code is expected. Next, `not new_value` leads to `_uc_slot_in_use(...) is True`, which is False for Available, so the result is `SlotCredential.empty()` (with '0000' the `strip('0')` branch gives empty too). `_confirm_slot(empty)` then fails a pending set, or empties confirmed data, and the slot re-syncs: the loop #863 guarded against. The new comment says the code field is read as a code there, but it isn't."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 427,
    "summary": "(b), fallout of 62e8a0fa plus 1649267e: zwave-js-ui sets with no Supervision result now return OPTIMISTIC and stay pending, and `observe_push` fails a pending set on any readable push of a different code. That includes the broker replaying the retained pre-write code, the replay 1649267e exempts only for clears.",
    "failure_scenario": "A zwave-js-ui push lock gets an unsupervised set of 2222 over 1111, so the set is pending and believed. Before the look reads, a reconnect or resubscribe (also from `_async_ensure_node_subscription` inside the hard refresh) replays the retained userCode=1111. `observe_push` takes the 'readable and different' branch: the set is failed, data shows 1111, the tick charges a strike and re-syncs. Repeated reconnects can suspend a slot whose code actually landed. On main this was CONFIRMED with nothing pending, so a replay could not charge."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 341,
    "summary": "(a): `record_clear` and `drop_superseded_by_clear` (through `drop_pending`) discard a failed-clear verdict the look already recorded but the tick hasn't taken, so a strike for a clear the lock ignored is lost.",
    "failure_scenario": "On a ZHA lock that ignores clears, an inactive slot gets a clear that is not confirmed. The look reads the readable code back and adds the slot to `_failed_writes`. Before the next sync tick, a direct clear (websocket `async_clear_usercode`) or a release runs on the same slot. `drop_superseded_by_clear` calls `drop_pending`, which discards the verdict, or the provider's `record_clear` discards it. The tick then never charges that strike, so suspending a lock that ignores clears takes extra cycles, without limit if out-of-tick clears keep arriving."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 419,
    "summary": "(a), altitude: the exemption 'a readable push leaves a pending clear to its read-back' is a coordinator-wide special case for one provider's transport quirk (zwave-js-ui replaying retained MQTT state). A deeper fix is to drop retained replays where the zwave-js-ui subscription receives them (HA's MQTT `ReceiveMessage.retain`).",
    "failure_scenario": "The cost: every push provider (ZHA, Matter, zwave_js) has a real readable push after a clear ignored until the next look. The same replay still fails pending sets (the second finding), because the special case covers only clears. Filtering retained replays in the zwave-js-ui push handler would fix both and remove the asymmetry from `observe_push`."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 162,
    "summary": "(a), reuse: the branch added `_raw_user_by_user_index` and `_lcm_slot_of_raw_user` but left `_lcm_slot_from_raw_users_by_credential_index` (name to `parse_tag`) and `_find_pin_credential_index_for_user` (lines 978-994, the `user_index` match) re-implementing them.",
    "failure_scenario": "The tag-to-slot and user-index lookups now exist twice. The credential-index fallback could return `_lcm_slot_of_raw_user(next((u for u in raw_users if any(...)), None))`, and `_find_pin_credential_index_for_user` could start from `_raw_user_by_user_index`. A future change to tag parsing or user matching (for example the `parse_tag` contract) has to be made twice or the copies drift."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1388,
    "summary": "(a), simplification: there are now three rate-limited read wrappers over the same two primitives (`async_internal_get_usercodes`, `async_internal_hard_refresh_codes`, `async_internal_read_back`), each sizing exchanges its own way. `async_internal_read_back(lambda: self.managed_slots, refresh=...)` covers both of the others.",
    "failure_scenario": "The poll, drift-check and look paths each carry their own exchange-budget formula (`len(managed_slots)` vs `len(managed_slots | slots)`). A future change to how a read is scoped or budgeted has to be made in three places. Collapsing them to one wrapper removes two `@final` methods without changing behavior."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js.py",
    "line": 939,
    "summary": "(a), efficiency (minor): `_handle_uc_code_update` now looks up the cached userIdStatus through `_uc_slot_status`, then `_uc_slot_in_use` calls `get_usercode`, which looks up the same status value again (plus the code value).",
    "failure_scenario": "Each userCode value update builds value-id strings and does dictionary lookups for the same status two or three times. Taking `in_use` from the status already read (`status == CodeSlotStatus.ENABLED`, which is `get_usercode`'s own rule) saves the repeat lookups and keeps one source for the status on this hot push path."
  }
]
```

