toolUseResult.result:
The revert is complete. `e9ac3d4a` is the exact line-for-line inverse of `ce805438` across the coordinator, the property model, the coordinator tests and the zwave-js-ui e2e test, and no comments or AGENTS.md text still describe failures being kept until the tick takes them.

`b389d58f` holds up. A stale retained status can only set the slot's enabled flag ahead of the live publish that corrects it. Per-connection MQTT ordering keeps the replay ahead of that live publish, so I found no failure. `4cd796b1` is a pure move with the same meaning, and no inline copies of the check remain. `cdc75502` is correct for the lookup it changed, but the other readers of a Matter user index still disagree about duplicate copies (findings 1 and 2).

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1711,
    "summary": "(b) Fallout of cdc75502. The LockUserChange dispatch decides the owner, its slot and whether it holds a PIN from only the first raw user at the index (`_raw_user_by_user_index`). cdc75502 just restored searching every copy in `_find_pin_credential_index_for_user` because a lock can list one index twice, and `async_get_users` counts PINs from every copy, so the stale-clear and stale-add rules again count a PIN differently from the other reads (the rule 6e375b42 set out to enforce).",
    "failure_scenario": "Index 5 is listed twice: the first copy tagged `lcm:3:` with no credentials, the second with the PIN. A late CLEAR for index 5 finds `holds_pin` False, so the stale-clear guard does not fire and slot 3 is confirmed empty while the lock still holds the PIN. A pending set is failed and charged, or sync re-sets a code that is already there. Mirror case: a genuine ADD/MODIFY is dropped as a 'stale add' because the first copy has no PIN."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 539,
    "summary": "(b) Altitude. Duplicate copies of a user index are resolved three ways: `async_get_users` anchors the index to the LAST copy's tag but counts PINs from ALL copies; the LockUserChange and LockOperation dispatches use the FIRST copy; the credential-index lookup searches ANY copy. Each consumer patches the duplicate case on its own, and they drift apart.",
    "failure_scenario": "Copies are [tagged `lcm:3:`, untagged]. `async_get_users` pops the anchor for the index (last copy is untagged), while the LockOperation dispatch (line 1507) resolves slot 3 from the first copy. The same index is LCM's for one reader and foreign for another, and a later CLEAR whose read fails has no anchor to fall back on. Merging the copies once in `_raw_lock_users` (or making every consumer go through `_raw_users_by_user_index`) gives one answer."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1706,
    "summary": "(a) The new `anchored_at_event` guard on the read-failure CLEAR fallback only notices an index moving to a DIFFERENT slot. If a write during the queued turn re-adds the SAME slot on the reused index, the anchor is unchanged, so the stale CLEAR is still applied and the fresh anchor is popped. Catching LockBusy (new, now that the read waits for the lock's turn) makes this fallback much easier to reach.",
    "failure_scenario": "LCM clears slot 3 (index 5), and the CLEAR dispatch's read queues behind a bulk sync. Sync then re-sets slot 3, the lock reuses index 5, and the anchor is still 3. The read ends in LockBusy, the anchors compare equal, and line 1748 pops index 5 and confirms slot 3 empty. The just-written code shows as absent (a pending set is failed and charged), and the next CLEAR on index 5 has no anchor."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1658,
    "summary": "(a) Efficiency. The shared LockUserChange read stops being shareable in `pre_execute`, but `_execute_locked` runs `pre_execute` before the rate-limit sleep (up to MIN_OPERATION_DELAY). An event arriving during that sleep starts a second full user read even though the first has not read yet and would cover it.",
    "failure_scenario": "During a bulk sync, each write's PIN-add event that lands in the 2 s rate-limit window after the shared read takes the turn queues another full `_raw_lock_users` walk behind the remaining writes: the read pile-up `_shared_user_read` was added to prevent. Stop sharing after the delay, just before `func` runs, instead."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1507,
    "summary": "(c) The LockOperation dispatch resolves the slot from the first raw user at `userIndex` only, so a lock that lists an index twice (the case cdc75502 handles) can attribute a keypad use to no slot or the wrong one.",
    "failure_scenario": "Index 5 copies: [untagged, tagged `lcm:3:`]. The first copy has no tag, so `code_slot` is None. The credential-index fallback then runs only if `credentialIndex` was sent; without it the keypad use of slot 3 is silently dropped and no code-slot event fires."
  },
  {
    "file": "AGENTS.md",
    "line": 507,
    "summary": "(a) The new 'Clears' guidance says a polled provider that gets a confirmed clear 'just returns True'. But zwave-js-ui's `async_delete_credential` pushes `SlotCredential.empty()` on a confirmed clear for api-only (polled) locks as well, and the base then also requests a refresh.",
    "failure_scenario": "A provider author following AGENTS.md for a polled lock that receives a Supervision Success will not push, while the reference implementation does. Providers then disagree on whether a confirmed clear updates the coordinator before the read-back, which affects whether a lagging read can briefly re-show the old code."
  }
]
```

