    "line": 1721,
    "summary": "(b) Fallout of the in-turn read (8ac6239f) and the anchor check (3e42c72e), kept by 661e0db1. When a CLEAR event's user read fails with LockDisconnected or LockOperationFailed, the fallback compares the anchor's slot value with what it was at event time. That comparison misses Lock Code Manager setting the same slot again, because a Matter clear removes only the credential and keeps the user, so index→slot never changes. The LockBusy branch's own comment describes exactly this hazard, but the transport-failure branch still falls back to the anchor and pops it.",
    "failure_scenario": "Slot 3 (user index 5) is toggled off and back on. The clear's LockUserChange arrives after the re-set has already queued, so the shared read waits out the set's turn. The set lands and the set_user UPDATE path (line 690) re-anchors 5→3. The read then times out (LockDisconnected after it got the turn). `_slot_by_user_index.get(5) == anchored_at_event == 3`, so line 1763 pops the anchor and `_confirm_slot(3, empty)` pushes empty over a code that is on the lock. The slot flips out of sync and is rewritten for nothing. A pending optimistic set is failed and charged a strike. The anchor is also dropped for a user that still exists. Fix: compare a per-index write generation counter instead of the slot value, or drop the fallback whenever the read waited behind another holder, as the LockBusy branch already does."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1694,
    "summary": "(a) 661e0db1 together with ea1052e0. Every LockUserChange whose shared read raises LockBusy is dropped, including an out-of-band CLEAR. Matter is a push provider whose only other reread is the hourly drift check (`hard_refresh_interval` = 1h), so 'the coordinator's own reads relearn the slot' can take up to an hour. An event that joined the shared read moments before it gave up is also dropped, even though it barely waited and a read of its own could have got the turn.",
    "failure_scenario": "A bulk sync keeps the turn busy past one holder's wait budget. Meanwhile someone deletes a managed user's PIN in the vendor app. The LockUserChange CLEAR's read gets LockBusy and is dropped, and nothing pushes empty. The slot reads in sync while the PIN is no longer on the lock, so that user is locked out until the next drift check up to an hour later. Before 661e0db1 the anchor fallback would have pushed empty and sync would have re-added the code."
  },
  {
    "file": "AGENTS.md",
    "line": 506,
    "summary": "(a) bab6243d. The new Clears contract lists Matter as a provider 'whose lock reports no change of its own for the clear' (matching the async_delete_credential docstring, 'Matter does not emit LockUserChange for LCM-initiated clears'). This branch's own Matter code assumes the opposite: `_shared_user_read` exists because 'a bulk sync sets off an event per write', and the LockBusy and anchor branches reason about the event from Lock Code Manager's own clear arriving after a re-set.",
    "failure_scenario": "A contributor follows the documented contract and treats Matter clears as having no lock-side event. They then remove or relax the stale-clear, anchor and LockBusy guards in `_dispatch_lock_user_change` as unneeded, which brings back empty pushes over landed sets. Both the provider contract and the docstring misstate behavior the code depends on."
  }
