content.content:
Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
Seven findings below, most severe first. One correctness defect predates the branch (c) and one is introduced by it (a). The other five are cleanup and test findings introduced by the branch. Nothing was written to the worktree.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 846,
    "summary": "(c) The options flow that edits an entry's locks never calls _check_common_slots or checks other entries' claims, so two entries can claim the same slot on one lock. This breaks the 'at most one entry' premise that find_entry_for_lock_slot's docstring states and that desired_credential now relies on.",
    "failure_scenario": "Entry A manages slots 1-3 on lock L. Entry B manages slots 1-3 on lock M, and the options flow adds L to B; only capacity and unclaimed-MQTT checks run, so it is accepted. find_entry_for_lock_slot(L, 1, keeping_slots=True) returns whichever entry async_entries lists first. B's code sensor for slot 1 on L, when the code is unreadable, shows A's PIN, and the stale-Available guard protects A's PIN rather than B's. Both entries also write slot 1."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/queries.py",
    "line": 116,
    "summary": "(a) _keeps_its_slots ignores load state on purpose, so an enabled entry that failed for good (SETUP_ERROR or MIGRATION_ERROR) still counts as wanting its slots, though it never syncs them.",
    "failure_scenario": "Entry B shares lock L and fails migration (for example, its stored version comes from a newer release after a downgrade), so it is enabled but MIGRATION_ERROR. Its slot 3 is cleared at the keypad and the lock sends userIdStatus=AVAILABLE. zwave_js._handle_uc_status_update and the zwave_js_ui handler see desired_credential(slot 3).is_present and drop the genuine report. The coordinator keeps showing slot 3 holding B's PIN until a hard refresh. On main, the creating entry A was asked, A does not hold slot 3, and the report was believed."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/queries.py",
    "line": 107,
    "summary": "(a) _keeps_its_slots re-implements Home Assistant's own async_entries(DOMAIN, include_disabled=False, include_ignore=False) filter. iter_loaded_lcm_entries in the same file already uses that filter.",
    "failure_scenario": "The cost is duplication: a second hand-written definition of 'enabled and not ignored' that can drift from Home Assistant's (Home Assistant tests `not entry.disabled_by`; this tests `is None`). The simpler form is `hass.config_entries.async_entries(DOMAIN, include_ignore=not keeping_slots, include_disabled=not keeping_slots)`, with the helper deleted. The SOURCE_IGNORE branch and its test also cover a state this integration cannot reach: it has no discovery step (only user, yaml and reauth), so no ignored Lock Code Manager entry can exist."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/coordinator.py",
    "line": 138,
    "summary": "(a) desired_credential was the only reader of self._config_entry. After this change the attribute is assigned but never read.",
    "failure_scenario": "The dead state misleads the next reader into thinking the coordinator still consults its creating entry, which is the exact bug class this branch fixes. DataUpdateCoordinator already stores config_entry. Remove the attribute."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/sensor.py",
    "line": 90,
    "summary": "(a) The code sensor already knows which entry owns its slot (self.config_entry), but the fallback now goes through a lookup over all entries. On a lock that masks codes, that lookup runs on every state write of every slot sensor.",
    "failure_scenario": "On a lock that reports '****' for every slot, every coordinator update runs find_entry_for_lock_slot for each slot sensor. For every entry that is not loaded (disabled, reloading or failed), that rebuilds EntryConfig.from_entry, and has_slot walks all users through name_for. On main this was one dict lookup. The cheaper form is to read get_entry_config(self.config_entry).slot(n) in the sensor, or let desired_credential take the owning entry when the caller has it."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/zwave_js/test_e2e.py",
    "line": 297,
    "summary": "(a) _entry_on_slot_3 builds entries in the legacy CONF_SLOTS data shape. In the mock_state tests (setting_up, unloading_to_reload, between_unload_and_setup) the entry is never set up or migrated, so they only exercise the pre-migration branch of EntryConfig.from_entry.",
    "failure_scenario": "The windows these tests are meant to cover are a current entry reloading, and that entry keeps its users in subentries. If from_entry's subentry path mis-resolved a slot while runtime_data is absent, a stale Available during an options reload would be believed and every one of these tests would still pass. The test supplies its own input instead of the shape production has."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zwave_js_ui.py",
    "line": 632,
    "summary": "(a) The branch says it fixes the stale-Available guard in zwave_js_ui too, but no test drives a shared lock through the zwave_js_ui MQTT userIdStatus=AVAILABLE path.",
    "failure_scenario": "That handler sets _slot_enabled[slot]=False before it consults desired_credential, and it has its own lock and coordinator wiring. A regression where it still misses the second entry's slot (an AVAILABLE on entry B's slot believed, the code reported empty, then rewritten every tick) would not be caught; only zwave_js has the shared-lock end-to-end test."
  }
]
```

