    "failure_scenario": "Entry A holds lock L1 and lock L2. Before this fix, A's slot 1 on L2 also overlapped with entry B's slot 1 there. Then L1's entity disappears and reauth opens. The user picks a replacement lock and keeps L2. They get slots_already_configured for L2 every time, because nothing they choose in this form changes A's numbers, so reauth cannot finish. Reauth also has no options flow to fall back on while the entry is failed. Scoping it like the options flow would remove the dead end: check only locks not in entry_config.locks."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 870,
    "summary": "(c) The options capacity check still reads every submitted lock, including ones already held, even though this form never changes the numbers. One undersized lock the entry already holds refuses every later edit, and every save wakes and queries every lock.",
    "failure_scenario": "A lock was swapped in through reauth while it was asleep, so async_check_slot_capacity skipped it (LockQuerySkipped). It reports 10 slots, and the entry already uses number 12. From then on, any options save fails with slot_out_of_range for that lock, including a save that only removes a different lock. The branch now gives that error readable text, but the user still has no way to save. Each save also queries every battery lock, not just the added one. Checking only newly added locks, as the branch does for overlap, would fix both."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 851,
    "summary": "(c) Adding a lock checks the entry's numbers against other entries' numbers on that lock, but not against unmanaged codes already on it. Allocation refuses to issue a number where it can read a code, so the options path skips that protection.",
    "failure_scenario": "Lock L has a code programmed at the keypad in slot 2 that no entry manages. Entry A, with users at 1 and 2, adds L through options. No other entry holds L, so the overlap check passes, and sync then writes A's user-2 PIN into slot 2. That overwrites a family member's own code with no warning. domain/unmanaged.py calls this 'the worse mistake'. Reauth's lock swap has the same gap. The branch's own comment ('numbers issued without looking at it') describes this case."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/strings.json",
    "line": 151,
    "summary": "(a) The slots_already_configured text copied into the options section tells the user to remove 'slots', but nothing reachable from this form can resolve the conflict. Lock Code Manager assigns the numbers and users keep them for as long as they exist.",
    "failure_scenario": "Entry A (users at 1 and 2) adds lock L, where entry B holds 1, 2 and 3. The form says slots 1 and 2 'can't be added to this configuration without being removed from the other one'. But the user didn't choose slots, they chose a lock. Re-saving, or adding or editing users, never renumbers A's existing users. The only ways out are to delete and re-create A's users after adding L, or to delete B's users, and the message names neither. The slot_out_of_range text added in the same hunk does explain this ('Lock Code Manager assigns those numbers itself, so the way to free them is to remove some users'). Root cause: the form refuses instead of renumbering the conflicting users on the added lock."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_config_flow.py",
    "line": 2520,
    "summary": "(a) test_options_flow_does_not_hold_an_entry_against_itself never exercises self-exclusion. Because only added locks are checked, the entry can never match itself, so the self.config_entry argument at config_flow.py:863 is dead on this path.",
    "failure_scenario": "Mutation run in a scratch copy: I removed the self.config_entry argument from the options-flow _check_common_slots call. All 11 options_flow tests still pass. The test submits only the locks the entry already holds, so the added-lock list is empty and the check returns immediately. Its docstring's claim ('counting the entry among the others would refuse every save') is unverified, and a regression that broke self-exclusion would not be caught."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 760,
    "summary": "(c) On a reauth error, description_placeholders.update(additional_placeholders) overwrites the reauth description's {lock}, which names the missing lock, with the lock from the error.",
    "failure_scenario": "Reauth opens for missing lock lock.front. The user submits lock.back, which trips slots_already_configured or slot_out_of_range. Both put 'lock': 'lock.back' in their placeholders. The re-rendered description now says 'The lock `lock.back` ... is no longer configured in Home Assistant and must be removed', naming a working lock the user just selected as the one that's gone."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 179,
    "summary": "(c) _check_common_slots reports only the first conflicting (lock, entry) pair, so adding several conflicting locks surfaces one refusal per submit.",
    "failure_scenario": "Entry A adds L2 and L3 in one options save. Entry B overlaps A's numbers on L2 and entry C overlaps them on L3. The form names only L2 / B. The user resolves it, resubmits, and only then learns about L3 / C. Each round costs a destructive user deletion, because the conflict can only be fixed by removing users (see the strings finding)."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 855,
    "summary": "(a) The 'only newly added locks' filtering is a list comprehension at the call site, while the neighbouring _check_unclaimed_mqtt_locks takes an already_configured parameter for the same rule. As a result, reauth (line 743) did not pick up the scoping.",
    "failure_scenario": "The same grandfathering rule is written two different ways, a helper parameter in one place and an inline filter in the other. The rule now lives in a single caller, so reauth still checks every lock (finding 1). Giving _check_common_slots the same already_configured parameter would let both callers share it and make the self-exclusion argument unnecessary."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 182,
    "summary": "(c) _check_common_slots re-implements get_managed_slots(hass, lock, excluding=entry) from domain/queries.py (the same scan of every entry and has_lock), and rebuilds {int(s) for s in slots_list} once per entry per lock.",
    "failure_scenario": "Two copies of the 'which numbers do other entries manage on this lock' query can drift. For example, allocation compares with `entry is not excluding` and this helper with `config_entry == entry`, so a fix to one, such as handling legacy-shape entries, won't reach the other. Only the entry title is unique to this helper. The set rebuild is minor wasted work inside the generator."
  }
]
```

